WEBVTT

NOTE Visual text from a silent video.

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:08.100
The task asks for overdue invoices to be totalled and saved as a draft, not sent.

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:15.500
In this example, 20 and 30 are overdue; the invoice for 10 is not.

3
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:24.000
The expected result is 50, with a saved draft and no message sent.

4
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:31.400
Both runs produce the same correct draft.

5
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:38.100
One uses three calls; the other repeats queries and consumes eight.

6
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:46.000
Compare trajectories for the same task, not only their final text.

7
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:53.400
The total can be correct and the cost can remain within budget.

8
00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:00.100
But sending a forbidden draft violates the task contract.

9
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:08.000
The evaluation must preserve that failure rather than average it into success.

10
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:15.400
Eighteen tasks take one second; two others take nine.

11
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:22.100
The mean is 1.8 seconds, but the 95th percentile is nine in this sample.

12
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:30.000
Inspect slow cases and their tools, not only the average.

13
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:37.400
A clear response can still claim that 20 plus 30 equals 60.

14
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:44.100
A deterministic verifier compares the number with the expected result: 50.

15
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:52.000
Use the objective condition when one exists; writing style cannot replace it.

16
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:59.400
A test can appear to pass if the agent can change its expected result.

17
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:06.100
Separate task permissions from the verifier’s resources.

18
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:14.000
Record out-of-scope attempts and inspect the trajectory.
